egl,zink: add an ohos platform for zink

OpenHarmony's EGL wrapper loads a vendor EGL and GLES. This EGL platform
makes Zink that EGL. Zink draws on the GPU of the first DRM render node
that the process can open. The OpenHarmony build has no Gallium driver
other than Zink, so the DRM pipe loader picks Zink for every device.

A window surface works as on the Android platform. For each frame, the
platform requests a buffer from the NativeWindow and imports the
buffer's dma-buf as a linear image with the handle's row pitch. In
eglSwapBuffers, it flushes the buffer back to the window with a sync
file that signals when the frame's rendering is done. The buffers have
no implicit synchronization. The platform keeps the image of each of the
window's buffers, so each buffer is imported once.

The window's queue allocates the buffers with the usage, format and size
that the window's owner set. render_service sets its screen's usage
before each frame. OpenHarmony's Vulkan swapchain layer sets the usage
that the Vulkan driver asks for, so a swapchain on render_service's
screen would get a new buffer for every frame. render_service also takes
its screen's buffer from the window as soon as eglSwapBuffers returns, so
the frame reaches the window inside the swap.

The window configs are RGBA, because OpenHarmony's windows are RGBA.
Mesa makes RGBA configs when the image loader reports
DRI_LOADER_CAP_RGBA_ORDERING.

render_service imports the buffers of other surfaces with
eglCreateImageKHR(EGL_NATIVE_BUFFER_OHOS) and a NativeWindowBuffer. The
platform imports them in the same way as window buffers.
render_service passes its context with this target, so the platform
ignores the context. The value of EGL_NATIVE_BUFFER_OHOS comes from
OpenHarmony's EGL headers.

Video frames are YUV 4:2:0 buffers from the same allocator, in NV12,
NV21, I420 or YV12. Their chroma planes follow the luma plane in the
same dma-buf, so the platform imports them as two or three planes of
that dma-buf. The buffers carry no color metadata, so they import as
BT.601 limited range with centered chroma, which is Android's gralloc
default.

The surface creation follows OpenHarmony's Mesa,
https://gitee.com/openharmony/third_party_mesa3d at
ea74a6339917390657db153cb9792f9ccac9f5ae, which is MIT-licensed like
Mesa.

